---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
---

<BaseLayout title="About" description="Why PrismaLens exists and the rules it is built on.">
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-2xl px-4 py-16 leading-relaxed sm:px-6 sm:py-24">
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight">About PrismaLens</h1>

    <p class="mt-8 text-lg">
      When an alert fires, the answer is usually already somewhere in your dashboards and your
      code. Finding it is the slow part: which deploy, which change, which line. Coding agents
      have become good at reading a codebase and following that kind of trail, and most engineers
      already have one installed and signed in.
    </p>

    <p class="mt-5 text-muted-foreground">
      PrismaLens puts that agent on the incident. It opens the incident from the alert, gives the
      agent a copy of the service's code, refuses anything that would change it, and keeps the
      whole run: the report, the evidence and every command the agent ran.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-14 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">The rules it follows</h2>

    <p class="mt-5 text-muted-foreground">
      <strong class="text-foreground">Evidence over scores.</strong> A report ranks the causes and
      shows what each one rests on. It never prints a confidence percentage. A number you cannot
      check is worse than no number.
    </p>

    <p class="mt-5 text-muted-foreground">
      <strong class="text-foreground">Your agent, your login.</strong> PrismaLens never calls a model
      and never reads your agent's credentials. There is no PrismaLens account and nothing to pay us.
    </p>

    <p class="mt-5 text-muted-foreground">
      <strong class="text-foreground">Say where the limits are.</strong> The read-only policy is a
      guardrail, not a wall, and the docs say so. Every report states whether the run was sandboxed.
    </p>

    <p class="mt-5 text-muted-foreground">
      <strong class="text-foreground">Yours to run.</strong> PrismaLens is Apache-2.0 and runs on your
      machine. Everything it keeps is in <code>~/.prismalens</code>, and it sends nothing to us.
    </p>

    <p class="mt-14">
      Feedback and bug reports go to
      <a href="https://github.com/prismalens/prismalens/issues" class="underline underline-offset-4">GitHub issues</a>.
      Code contributions open up a little later.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
